so let me see if I understand. for you a "feature" is a row in the data frame.
and you have  a set of functions that operate on features (e.g. the input of each of these function is a feature, and the output is a single value.
suppose your functions are f1, f2,...,fn and your data.frame is called df, then your features are df[1,], df[2,], and so on. and your outcome should be a data.frame
 that contains has the following data:

f1(df[1,])    f2(df[1,])   ...    fn(df[1,])
f1(df[2,])    f2(df[2,])   ...    fn(df[2,])
etc...

in general:
result[i,j] = fj(df[i,])

is that true?
do you need for any of this the whole data frame to be passed as parameter to your basis functions? (note - it is expensive to pass data frames as parameters - every time you do that the data frame is copied! if your data frame is big, that is very costly)

